#: src/macros/intro/why.md:6
msgid "What Are Macros For"
msgstr ""

#: src/macros/intro/why.md:8
msgid ""
"Macros are a powerful tool, but carry their own complexity and should be "
"used only when worthwhile. They are used to solve problems that cannot be "
"easily solved with standard Rust language features:"
msgstr ""

#: src/macros/intro/why.md:12
msgid ""
"**Reducing Boilerplate:** Implementing traits via `#[derive(...)]` or "
"reducing syntactic repetition."
msgstr ""

#: src/macros/intro/why.md:14
msgid ""
"**Variadic Interfaces:** Functions that take a variable number of arguments "
"of different types (like `println!` and `vec!`)."
msgstr ""

#: src/macros/intro/why.md:16
msgid ""
"**Domain-Specific Languages (DSLs):** Creating custom syntax inside Rust "
"(e.g., `println!`'s format strings, or macros that statically expand HTML "
"templates or parse SQL queries)."
msgstr ""

#: src/macros/intro/why.md:19
msgid ""
"**Compile-Time Computation:** Inspecting configuration files, environment "
"variables, or data at build time (e.g., `include_str!`, `env!`)."
msgstr ""

#: src/macros/intro/why.md:22
msgid ""
"Because each macro can define its own input syntax and can perform "
"operations not available to normal Rust code, macros can make code harder to "
"read than it would be otherwise. Try to avoid using macros when the same "
"abstraction can be achieved in another way, and when defining macros, try to "
"align with syntactic and naming patterns followed in non-macro Rust code."
msgstr ""

#: src/macros/intro/why.md:31
msgid ""
"Contrast with limitations of normal Rust code: functions have a fixed "
"signature (fixed number of arguments of specific types) and cannot generate "
"new types or modules."
msgstr ""
